#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531682} :activity/id #uuid "9212bfd3-0830-5e35-a82a-a827c5e76176" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531682 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092923 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a7c6d33-ec9d-5f09-8a41-23c7079e0681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091742} {:answer/id #uuid "635d1f78-1ff1-548c-b1d2-727e82d8d662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531082} {:answer/id #uuid "ad493751-d3c8-5246-8a30-3a2a4aefa677" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531239} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a466dc-34ab-5292-b042-6078d2d70c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /2}-{e}^{-\\pi /2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531253} {:answer/id #uuid "d7923dd0-6a1a-51d5-bb60-8f501bdb44b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\pi /2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531306}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531239} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4db8ae4-ea41-5588-80ee-f64b1cf5f458" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d05250f7-7ca4-5099-862d-2ef2f12670bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-9536" :question/problem "![](images/fa/fa58c9d1c0680dd94bb25c04e0a268df836e14bfb217f0a20d3bd75224cec4e8.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r={e}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{({e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2\\theta }+{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /2}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "34a6170e-0575-571a-ae56-b0c6b66f198b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531712}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531689 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531603 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6dfc676-da5c-574c-a05b-9ad750d0d173" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531255}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531255} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5c4df65-6015-5ec9-a590-2b33b6d37d1f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6a23334e-1f04-5511-928e-2caa0a2313aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-344426" :question/problem "The exact global minimum value of the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-4x+5$ on the closed interval $0\\le x\\le 5$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)={x}^{2}-4x+5,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2x-4.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4 & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=2$ and the endpoints $x=0,5$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints)\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & ={0}^{2}-4(0)+5=5 \\\\ f(2) & ={2}^{2}-4(2)+5=1 \\\\ f(5) & ={5}^{2}-4(5)+5=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(2)=1,$ so the global minimum is $1.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(5)=10,$ so the global maximum is $10.$\n\n![](images/9a/9a52ee757248f6b742af36af1e6af9e801cb8e5276b119e399b6fff36d75d0dd.png)"} :step/id #uuid "57b8dfd6-9bac-59cf-ac44-c16486eda1c3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531709}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531702 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51767ce2-04dc-5c92-be64-ecdd456e4d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248156} {:answer/id #uuid "5af05724-0a68-584c-bdcc-e0f997546e41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248248} {:answer/id #uuid "a32c58bd-145a-53ec-98dc-145783470c2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248884} {:answer/id #uuid "b0502d42-a25a-5ae1-9cca-dcf3a4cbe860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249008} {:answer/id #uuid "c64da03c-c464-59cb-81c9-d62f5d957afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249187}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249008} :answer-field/id #uuid "a22fa253-1174-584a-9758-f3f964e75c1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "91292256-4f82-5669-95d9-e91e8b8d6af8" :question/math-academy-id "q-114552" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 2x{log}_{5}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{5}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} \\\\ {v}^{′}=2x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x{log}_{5}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={log}_{5}⁡(x)({x}^{2})-\\int ({x}^{2})(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{log}_{5}⁡(x)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{log}_{5}⁡(x)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{5}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.$"} :step/id #uuid "9cb89aa4-e9c5-5f00-b04f-4e9bc062f9b4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531720}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531709 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354291 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6447923e-3191-5c08-b560-016a9cca81ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469362} {:answer/id #uuid "0cc433cf-3e13-565e-b11d-47ebc6009d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469363}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469363} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cdb2ef9-a7f4-5197-a4be-9be3babb8318" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470293} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6f89934-e7fc-5003-8d75-d813ae4eddf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469364} {:answer/id #uuid "bb820a21-e923-56ac-bab5-b0c3ae2a331f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469365}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469365} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8b6b289-f586-5a9d-a28a-19624f5169ef" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470294} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd2a7885-0052-528b-b38b-7b898ccca0b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469366} {:answer/id #uuid "f9179f60-52cd-5391-9242-248f117920a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469367}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469366} :answer-field/id #uuid "d303017f-d35d-596d-a193-c2ff0482d558" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d2810540-9b59-5736-808a-d6bd469fdb33" :question/math-academy-id "q-8415" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(0,0),(2,0),(2,2).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n- **I.** $(0,0)$\n- **II.** $(2,-2)$\n- **III.** $(-2,0)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${270}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{270}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{270}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(0,0),(0,-2),(2,-2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "b55407ec-a2ee-5f9b-90f8-39bfad1179ca"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531712 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531568 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c088d0f-ace9-54be-ac56-eec3807e7304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=1\\,800P(0.2+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531066} {:answer/id #uuid "5e283cf8-0d6b-5c7b-924b-0c460fd57833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=1\\,800P(0.2-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531071} {:answer/id #uuid "75cc83f0-cddd-51ed-9a88-cb259f07d3eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P(1\\,800+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531124} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b62c6a-1496-5d36-a0b2-51a726ef8705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P(1\\,800-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531296} {:answer/id #uuid "d85d5e68-caf7-5fe3-b3d9-26adeac92153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=-0.2P(1\\,800-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531308}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531296} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb8637b4-c23b-5591-8ea3-4b43b99fdc95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2014fae0-a89c-5d1e-8735-16f0159dd06c" :question/math-academy-id "q-119283" :question/problem "The growth rate of a watermelon production $P(t)$ in a certain land is jointly proportional to the watermelon production at time $t,$ measured in months, and the difference between the land's carrying capacity and the watermelon production. If the constant of proportionality is $0.2$ and the maximum possible watermelon production is $1\\,800,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the watermelon production growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the watermelon production at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the watermelon production, the watermelon production follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.2,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=1\\,800.$ Therefore, the watermelon production is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P(1\\,800-P).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ba33e601-868b-5386-9000-5cc1fbdaddc2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531702}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531720 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531587 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37b64633-9200-5ac4-9020-49356273a990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530986} {:answer/id #uuid "4fab1f17-f040-5e8d-ba53-5a0cfda68a34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{4}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531036} {:answer/id #uuid "693cf72a-54a9-5ffe-b86a-e3d60d8e9126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531097} {:answer/id #uuid "a8cf6697-f373-540e-8e34-c5fc49f71fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531230} {:answer/id #uuid "bb77564e-76df-5e96-b697-013fee021596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{4}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531261}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531036} :answer-field/id #uuid "632462c4-14ed-5c49-b96d-6314fe36a641" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "42788b85-30ff-5f36-a9fd-86e424b97617" :question/math-academy-id "q-138980" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,y={sin}^{3}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{3}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{3}⁡t)=3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t)}^{2}+{(3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{4}⁡t+9{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\cdot |3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{4}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dc9f379f-4b46-590e-aa26-910ad4ef4790" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531689}}] :activity/title "Quiz 1" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045460 :db/ident :activity.type/assessment} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530859}]}
